========================
     GIT MERGING
========================

What is Git Merge?

Git merge is used to combine changes from one branch
into another branch.

Usually, we merge a feature branch into the main branch.

Example:

main
  |
  |------ feature-login

After merging:

main
  |
  |------ feature-login changes


========================
   TWO TYPES OF MERGE
========================

1. FAST-FORWARD MERGE

A fast-forward merge happens when the main branch has
not changed after the new branch was created.

Git simply moves the main branch pointer forward.

Example:

main ---- A ---- B

                 \
feature ---------- C

After merge:

main ---- A ---- B ---- C

No separate merge commit is usually needed.


2. THREE-WAY MERGE

A three-way merge happens when both branches have
their own changes.

Git combines the changes from both branches.

Example:

        --- feature changes
       /
main --
       \
        --- main changes

Git creates a new merge commit to combine them.


========================
      MERGE CONFLICT
========================

A merge conflict happens when Git cannot automatically
decide how to combine changes.

Example:

Two branches change the same line of the same file.

Git will stop the merge and ask the developer to fix it.

Steps:

1. Open the conflicting file.
2. Decide which changes to keep.
3. Remove the conflict markers.
4. Save the file.
5. Add and commit the changes.


========================
     MERGE COMMAND
========================

First switch to the branch where you want
the changes to go.

Example:

git checkout main

Then merge the feature branch:

git merge branch-name

Example:

git merge feature-login


========================
    DELETE A BRANCH
========================

After merging, if the feature branch is no longer needed,
we can delete it.

git branch -d branch-name

Example:

git branch -d feature-login


